luation","slug":"monitoring_and_evaluation","type":"overview","sources":["Climate Watch"]},"64372":{"name":"Co-benefits","slug":"cobenefits","type":"overview","sources":["Climate Watch"]},"64373":{"name":"International Frameworks","slug":"international_frameworks","type":"overview","sources":["Climate Watch"]},"64374":{"name":"Gender and Indigenous People","slug":"gender_and_indigenous_people","type":"overview","sources":["Climate Watch"]},"64375":{"name":"Loss and Damage","slug":"loss_and_damage","type":"overview","sources":["Climate Watch"]},"64376":{"name":"Loss and Damage","slug":"loss_and_damage","type":"map","sources":["Climate Watch"]},"64377":{"name":"Transformative Adaptation","slug":"transformative_adaptation","type":"overview","sources":["Climate Watch"]},"64378":{"name":"Transformative Adaptation","slug":"transformative_adaptation","type":"map","sources":["Climate Watch"]},"64379":{"name":"Children and Young People","slug":"children_and_young_people","type":"overview","sources":["UNICEF"]},"64380":{"name":"Vulnerability","slug":"vulnerability","type":"overview","sources":["UNICEF","NDC Explorer"]},"64381":{"name":"Climate Risks","slug":"climate_risks","type":"overview","sources":["NDC Explorer"]},"64382":{"name":"Mitigation: GHG Target Type","slug":"mitigation_ghg_target_type","type":"map","sources":["Climate Watch"]},"64383":{"name":"Mitigation: Sector Coverage","slug":"mitigation_sector_coverage","type":"map","sources":["Climate Watch"]},"64384":{"name":"Mitigation: Gas Coverage","slug":"mitigation_gas_coverage","type":"map","sources":["Climate Watch"]},"64385":{"name":"Fairness, Ambition and Objective","slug":"fairness_ambition_and_objective","type":"overview","sources":["Climate Watch"]}},"sectors":{},"indicators":[{"id":648348,"source":"Climate Watch","name":"NDC summary","slug":"indc_summary","grouping_indicator":false,"category_ids":[64321,64324],"labels":{},"locations":{"IDN":[{"value":"\"Under the LCCP scenarios, Indonesia is expected to achieve its peak emissions by 2030, with estimated emission levels of 1,345,707 Gg CO2e under the LCCP_L scenario and 1,491,474 Gg CO2e under the LCCP_H scenario. Compared to the ENDC CM2 scenario, which projects emissions of 1,632,000 Gg CO2e in 2030, Indonesia's emissions are expected to be 8 - 17.5% lower. The emissions will decline in 2035 with the projection of GHG emission levels reaching 1,257,717 Gg CO2e under LCCP_L and 1,488,866 Gg CO2e under LCCP_H.\"","document_slug":"second_ndc","group_index":null},{"value":"\"Indonesia submits Enhanced NDC to the UNFCCC Secretariat by 23 September 2022 with increased emission reduction target from 29% in First NDC and Updated NDC to 31.89% unconditionally and from 41% in the Updated NDC to 43.20% conditionally. This Enhanced NDC is the transition towards Indonesia’s Second NDC which will be aligned with the Long-Term Low Carbon and Climate Resilience Strategy (LTS-LCCR) 2050 with a vision to achieve net-zero emission by 2060 or sooner.\"","document_slug":"revised_first_ndc","group_index":null},{"value":"\u003cp\u003e\"Indonesia has voluntarily committed to reduce unconditionally 26% of its greenhouse gases against the business as usual scenario by the year 2020.\"\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"Indonesia has committed to reduce unconditionally 29% of its greenhouse gasses emissions against the business as usual scenario by the year of 2030.\"\"Indonesia could increase its contribution up to 41% reduction of emissions by 2030, subject to availability of international support for finance, technology transfer and development and capacity building.\"\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIndonesia NDC also includes a section on adaptation.\u003c/p\u003e","document_slug":"first_ndc","group_index":null},{"value":"Indonesia has committed to reduce unconditionally 26% of its greenhouse gases against the business as usual scenario by the year 2020...Indonesia is committed to reducing emissions by 29% compared to the business as usual (BAU) scenario by 2030.\u003c/br\u003eConditional target: \"Indonesia's target should encourage support from international cooperation, which is expected to help Indonesia to increase its contribution up to 41% reduction in emissions by 2030.\"\u003cbr\u003eIndonesia also submitted an Annex on \"Indonesia Climate Resiliance Strategy\", including â€œIndonesia's Vulnerability to Climate Changeâ€ and â€œPriority Actions for Climate Resilience.\"","document_slug":"indc","group_index":null}]}},{"id":648353,"source":"Climate Watch","name":"GHG target","slug":"ghg_target","grouping_indicator":false,"category_ids":[64321,64326],"labels":{},"locations":{"IDN":[{"value":"\"Under the LCCP scenarios, Indonesia is expected to achieve its peak emissions by 2030, with estimated emission levels of 1,345,707 Gg CO2e under the LCCP_L scenario and 1,491,474 Gg CO2e under the LCCP_H scenario. Compared to the ENDC CM2 scenario, which projects emissions of 1,632,000 Gg CO2e in 2030, Indonesia's emissions are expected to be 8 - 17.5% lower. The emissions will decline in 2035 with the projection of GHG emission levels reaching 1,257,717 Gg CO2e under LCCP_L and 1,488,866 Gg CO2e under LCCP_H.\"","document_slug":"second_ndc","group_index":null},{"value":"Indonesia commits to reducing its emissions by 31.89% from BAU by 2030 (unconditional), and by 43.2% from BAU by 2030 (conditional).","document_slug":"revised_first_ndc","group_index":null},{"value":"29% (unconditional) and up to 41% (conditional) by 2030 compared to the business as usual scenario; 26% (unconditional) reduction in GHG emissions by 2020 compared to business as usual scenario","document_slug":"first_ndc","group_index":null},{"value":"29% (unconditional) and up to 41% (conditional) by 2030 compared to the business as usual scenario; 26% (unconditional) reduction in GHG emissions by 2020 compared to business as usual scenario","document_slug":"indc","group_index":null}]}},{"id":648356,"source":"Climate Watch","name":"Target year (mitigation)